 proof of Lemma 3.4 explained</a> <br><a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"a 'simple' case\" data-href=\"#a 'simple' case\" href=\"sep.4.html#a_'simple'_case_0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">a 'simple' case</a>\n<br><a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"general cases\" data-href=\"#general cases\" href=\"sep.4.html#general_cases_0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">general cases</a> <br><a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"references\" data-href=\"#references\" href=\"sep.4.html#references_0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">references</a>\n<br>\nThe main theme of this session is §1-§3 of <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/9801039\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" class=\"external-link is-unresolved\" href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/9801039\" target=\"_self\">Thurson's minimal stretch</a>.\nDefinition\nFor hyperbolic structures on the surface ,\n. and .\nTrivial facts:\n; satisfies the triangle inequality. is not symmetric in general.\nConsider the genus 2 surfaces with very long 'neck', the ratio of length of neck is while the ratio of perimeter of the 'neck' is , so the Lipschitz constants are different. and iff .\nThe proof is by area argument (use partition of unity for general cases).\nIs it true for coned case?\nAny possible restriction?\nWhy?\nTheorem 3.1 ( positive)\nFor , .\n<br>Try to understand the proof of <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"Sep.4 > ^8a2ec2\" data-href=\"Sep.4#^8a2ec2\" href=\"sep.4.html#^8a2ec2\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Theorem 3.1</a>.The next topic is to show is attained by simple closed curves.\nThe case of a pair of pants is typical, and implies the general case.Lemma 3.4 (shrinking at the waist)\nLet be hyperbolic structures on a pair of pants. If for which is not homotopic to the boundary, then .\n<br>Assume <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"Sep.4 > ^05f4b0\" data-href=\"Sep.4#^05f4b0\" href=\"sep.4.html#^05f4b0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Lemma 3.4</a>. If is not simple, with some self-intersection at , then there is some immersed figure-8, say . The covering space of corresponding to the group is a pair of pants.\nThe lift of in is not homotopic to the boundary (the 2 generators both appear), thus the sup is not attained. Go back to , the quotient of the boundary element gives a larger value of .This gives the Proposition 3.5 (supremum simple).<br>Try to understand the proof of <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"Sep.4 > ^05f4b0\" data-href=\"Sep.4#^05f4b0\" href=\"sep.4.html#^05f4b0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Lemma 3.4</a>. Remark\nThe rough idea of the proof is to find some element of maximal stretch, where the Lipschitz number can be attained.\nThis coincides with the latter proof of in the case of maximal stretch lamination.\nMore topics, including determining a hyperbolic structure by the length (ordered or not) of geodesic curves.Consider 4-coned sphere with angles , Gauss-Bonnet gives , so a natural restriction is .\nTo use the area argument for , the area is important.Example\nMap an ideal triangle to a non-ideal triangle. <br><img alt=\"IMG_0392.jpg\" src=\"assets/img_0392.jpg\" target=\"_self\">For example, we can extend the sides of the non-ideal triangle to the boundary, and connect the boundary point, to get an ideal triangle. Up to a Möbius transform, the problem is to 'shrink' an ideal triangle to a smaller triangle.In the upper half plane model, one example is and One possible way is the nearest point projection.\nFACT: in a CAT(0) space, the nearest point projection is 1-Lipschitz (consider a quadrilateral comparison, there are 2 obtuse angles!)We can actually calculate the expression of n.p.p. One possible way is map the circle connecting to , and then project to .How to find a homeomorphism which is -Lipschitz?\nI try the maps of the form , for example, let be the distance between circles, it fails as . Also, the map with as , it seems not working also.\ncone\nif consider the rotation around , the matrix should be gluing 2 sides of a fan of angle to get a cone of angle cusp\n, under the quotient, the horoball becomes a cusp\nfunnel\n, the length of neck is Theorem 3.1 ( positive)\nFor , .\nFor , we want to find some geodesic, which is shorter in than in .The metrics have the same area, so there is an area-preservingn (in the sense of area measure) diffeomorphism . Lift to . For any point , define the function .Lemma 3.2 ( unbounded)\nIf has an upper bound, then .\nRemark: Up to some Möbius transform, the unbounded-ness does not depend on .<br>\nProof of <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"Sep.4 > ^5890a3\" data-href=\"Sep.4#^5890a3\" href=\"sep.4.html#^5890a3\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Lemma 3.2</a>. Up to some Möbius transform, we can assume and , in the unit disk model. Fact: the area of hyperbolic ball is as .\nProof. The Euclidean radius satisfies , i.e. . So the area is Consider a wedge between two geodesic rays from to the boundary, they are mapped to two quasi-geodesic rays by . These quasi-geodesic rays are of bounded distance from two geodesic rays. Note such distance only depend on Lipschitz constant of on .<br>Definition (quasi-geodesic, <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"references\" data-href=\"#references\" href=\"sep.4.html#references_0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">BH</a> p.142)\nA -quasi-geodesic in is a -quasi-isometric embedding (where is either an interval in or the intersection of an interval with ). Explicitly, for any . For , is called a quasi-geodesic-ray.\n<br>Proposition (stability of quasi-geodesics, <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"references\" data-href=\"#references\" href=\"sep.4.html#references_0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">BH</a> p.401)\nFor all , there exists some const s.t.\nIf is a -hyperbolic geodesic space, is a -quasi-geodesic, and is a geodesic segment joining the endpoints of , then the Hausdorff distance between and is less than .\n(the proof is not easy, some one call it a \"Morse lemma\")\n<br><img alt=\"pasted-image-1789118452477.jpg\" src=\"assets/pasted-image-1789118452477.jpg\" target=\"_self\">Consider a wedge of angle , and suppose the image gives a geodesic wedge (connecting and the limiting points) of angle .Suppose is bounded by , and the Hausdorff above is bounded by The area of a fan of side length is , and by assumption, the area of the image is the same. is contained in a fan of angle and side length and tubes around the geodesic segments of side length . As , the area control is approximately<br><img alt=\"pasted-image-1789142100095.jpg\" src=\"assets/pasted-image-1789142100095.jpg\" target=\"_self\">(note the area of a tube is calculated as follows: in the upper half plane, the tube is , and the area is )for , as , is bounded above by some positive const. For the extension on , this means is Lipschitz.<br>Proposition (<a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"references\" data-href=\"#references\" href=\"sep.4.html#references_0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">McMullen</a> p.169)\nLet be a compact hyperbolic surfaces, be a homeomorphism which is invariant under the group action. If exists at some point , then is a Möbius transform.\nProof. By assumption, for some isomorphism , , for any . Up to a Möbius transform, consider the upper half plane model where , and .\nTake affine map on , then exists at 0 means that uniformly on . For each , take some s.t. are bounded, then they converge to some . So implies , which is a Möbius transform.Since is Lipschitz, it is differentiable a.e., the proposition above shows that is a Möbius transform, which gives an isometry between and .Lemma 3.3 (approximate orbits transitive)\nLet be a volume-preserving vector field on a manifold of finite volume which generate a flow for all time. Then for any compact and , there is some const s.t. any two points in are connected by an -approximate flow line in time .\nThe proof is by Poincaré recurrence and compactness.<br>Assume <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"Sep.4 > ^5890a3\" data-href=\"Sep.4#^5890a3\" href=\"sep.4.html#^5890a3\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Lemma 3.2</a> and <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"Sep.4 > ^852dd0\" data-href=\"Sep.4#^852dd0\" href=\"sep.4.html#^852dd0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Lemma 3.3</a>. Take any with large enough. We can assume the quotient is in the thick part of , since is an isometry around any cusp.<br>\nConsider all the extensions of the geodesic segment which have curvature less than some small constant , using approximate flow lines (it will not go back for the curvature condition). By <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"Sep.4 > ^852dd0\" data-href=\"Sep.4#^852dd0\" href=\"sep.4.html#^852dd0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Lemma 3.3</a>, there is an extension with finite length, which ends at . Note that on (the thick part of) , such the extra length have a uniform bound, say .\nNow we get a closed curve for with curvature less than , so it should be homotopic to a geodesic for of almost the same length , which satisfies . Now pull back to get a curve on which contains the geodesic segment , the geodesic length in its homotopic class satisfies . So for large , we have , which gives .Consider the case that takes the same value for the three boundary components.\nDecompose a pair of pants into two ideal triangle spiraling around the boundary components.<br>\n(the gluing is explained in <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"references\" data-href=\"#references\" href=\"sep.4.html#references_0\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Papadopoulos</a>)\n<br><img alt=\"pasted-image-1789546821679.jpg\" src=\"assets/pasted-image-1789546821679.jpg\" target=\"_self\">Consider the stretch by factor , the resulting ideal triangles glue together to form a new pair of pants whose boundary components are expended by the factor . This determines a Lipschitz map with Lipschitz constant Fact: the maximal stretch happens only for the directions parallel to the boundary\nProof. Consider the upper half plane model, where the stretch for the upper part is given by . The differential is , which implies that only the -direction is stretched.\nSo for any interior geodesic , , and is attained for geodesics in the direction of stretch.For general case, where takes different valued for the boundary components, let be the maximum, and construct as above an -Lipschitz map into the pair of pants whose boundary components are equally expanded by .\nWe then shrink the pair of pants by cutting out some strips in the completion.\nTo be continued in the next record.[BH] Bridson, Martin R., and André Haefliger.&nbsp;\"Metric spaces of non-positive curvature\". Springer Science &amp; Business Media, 2013. [McMullen] McMullen, Curtis. \"Ergodic theory, geometry and dynamics.\" [Papadopoulos] Papadopoulos, Athanase. \"Ideal triangles, hyperbolic surfaces and the Thurston metric on Teichmüller space.\"&nbsp;arXiv preprint arXiv:2103.10066,&nbsp;2021. (for 'citations', we hereby use Chicago style on google scholar)","aliases":[],"inlineTags":[],"frontmatterTags":[],"headers":[{"heading":"overview","level":1,"id":"overview_0"},{"heading":"coned case","level":1,"id":"coned_case_0"},{"heading":"the proof of Theorem 3.1 explained","level":1,"id":"the_proof_of_Theorem_3.1_explained_0"},{"heading":"<span class=\"math math-inline is-loaded\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\"><mj